WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.367
More agent world extras.

2
00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:06.833
Eight terms about operating, remembering, building, and naming agents.

3
00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:12.984
AgentOps is the emerging ops discipline for monitoring agents in production.

4
00:00:12.984 --> 00:00:15.133
It is also the name of a product.

5
00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:19.467
It tracks trajectories, tool failure rates, cost per task, and drift.

6
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:22.767
Think of it as your agent metrics, operationalized.

7
00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:29.500
Working memory, or a scratchpad, holds the agent's temporary reasoning state within a task.

8
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.600
That means notes, intermediate results, and plans.

9
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.735
It is distinct from short and long-term memory,

10
00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:39.233
and is often literally a scratchpad file or a state field.

11
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:44.200
The swarm pattern is a decentralized multi-agent design.

12
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.367
Hierarchical designs have a supervisor directing the agents.

13
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.809
In a swarm there is no supervisor.

14
00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:53.267
Agents hand off directly to each other.

15
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:57.533
Semantic memory is the facts I know memory type.

16
00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:03.800
It holds stable knowledge about the user and the world, and it is the store behind long-term memory.

17
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.067
Episodic memory, by contrast, is what happened.

18
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:12.333
Together with working memory, this trio is the standard agent-memory vocabulary.

19
00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:17.600
The Assistants API was OpenAI's hosted agent runtime.

20
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:22.233
Threads, file search, and the code interpreter were all managed server-side.

21
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:24.864
Deprecated in twenty twenty five,

22
00:01:24.864 --> 00:01:31.007
it shut down in August twenty twenty six in favour of the Responses API and Agents SDK.

23
00:01:31.007 --> 00:01:34.467
You'll still meet it in older code and tutorials.

24
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:43.033
Langflow, Flowise, Dify, and n8n are low-code and visual builders for LLM workflows and automations.

25
00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:46.733
They are great for fast proofs of concept and internal tools.

26
00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:51.600
Engineers should know them, know their ceilings, and know when to graduate to code.

27
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.900
These are the 2022 and 2023 ancestors of today's agents.

28
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.933
AutoGPT and BabyAGI popularized autonomous loops.

29
00:02:03.233 --> 00:02:05.433
Toolformer taught models to call tools.

30
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:08.233
And MRKL sketched a router over experts.

31
00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:11.233
They coined the patterns you now build properly.

32
00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:16.800
An AI gateway is the proxy layer all LLM traffic flows through.

33
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.933
Your apps send every request to it.

34
00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:22.411
It handles routing, fallbacks, rate limits, budgets,

35
00:02:22.411 --> 00:02:25.833
and tracing in one place, before reaching the providers.

36
00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:30.433
The names are LiteLLM proxy, Portkey, and Kong AI gateway.

37
00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:32.457
Let's recap.

38
00:02:32.457 --> 00:02:35.133
AgentOps monitors agents in production.

39
00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:38.933
Know the memory types: semantic, episodic, and working.

40
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:42.933
Swarms have no supervisor, and low-code builders have ceilings.

41
00:02:43.233 --> 00:02:47.167
And an AI gateway centralizes routing, budgets, and tracing.
